Lab 2: Install and Configure Red Hat OpenShift Platform Plus
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

• Install and configure Red Hat OpenShift Platform Plus on Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) • Connect multiple Kubernetes clusters to the Platform Plus instance • Navigate and verify the installation through the web interface • Understand the architecture and components of OpenShift Platform Plus • Manage multicluster environments using centralized governance

Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

• Basic understanding of Kubernetes concepts and architecture • Familiarity with Linux command line operations • Knowledge of container technologies and OpenShift fundamentals • Understanding of YAML configuration files • Basic networking concepts including DNS and load balancing • Experience with web-based administration interfaces

Lab Environment Setup
Ready-to-Use Cloud Machines: Al Nafi provides pre-configured Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your environment - no need to build your own virtual machines or configure infrastructure.

Your lab environment includes: • One RHEL 9 machine for OpenShift Platform Plus installation • Two additional RHEL 9 machines simulating remote cluster environments • Pre-installed Docker and necessary dependencies • Network connectivity between all machines

Task 1: Set up OpenShift Platform Plus on RHEL
Subtask 1.1: Prepare the System Environment
First, we need to prepare our RHEL system with the necessary prerequisites and dependencies.

Step 1: Update the system and install required packages

# Update the system packages
sudo dnf update -y

# Install required packages
sudo dnf install -y wget curl git podman skopeo jq

# Install Docker if not already present
sudo dnf config-manager --add-repo https://download.docker.com/linux/rhel/docker-ce.repo
sudo dnf install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io
sudo systemctl enable --now docker
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
Step 2: Configure system resources and limits

# Set up system limits for container operations
echo "* soft nofile 65536" | sudo tee -a /etc/security/limits.conf
echo "* hard nofile 65536" | sudo tee -a /etc/security/limits.conf

# Configure kernel parameters
echo "vm.max_map_count=262144" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
sudo sysctl -p
Step 3: Create dedicated user and directories

# Create OpenShift Platform Plus user
sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash ocp-plus
sudo usermod -aG docker ocp-plus

# Create necessary directories
sudo mkdir -p /opt/openshift-platform-plus
sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/openshift-platform-plus
sudo chown -R ocp-plus:ocp-plus /opt/openshift-platform-plus
sudo chown -R ocp-plus:ocp-plus /var/lib/openshift-platform-plus
Subtask 1.2: Download and Install OpenShift Platform Plus Components
Step 1: Download the required binaries and tools

# Switch to the ocp-plus user
sudo su - ocp-plus

# Set up environment variables
export OCP_PLUS_VERSION="4.14"
export INSTALL_DIR="/opt/openshift-platform-plus"

# Download OpenShift CLI tools
cd $INSTALL_DIR
wget https://mirror.openshift.com/pub/openshift-v4/clients/ocp/stable/openshift-client-linux.tar.gz
tar -xzf openshift-client-linux.tar.gz
sudo mv oc kubectl /usr/local/bin/
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/oc /usr/local/bin/kubectl
Step 2: Install Advanced Cluster Management (ACM) operator components

# Create configuration directory
mkdir -p $INSTALL_DIR/config

# Create ACM installation configuration
cat > $INSTALL_DIR/config/acm-install.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: open-cluster-management
---
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1
kind: OperatorGroup
metadata:
  name: open-cluster-management
  namespace: open-cluster-management
spec:
  targetNamespaces:
  - open-cluster-management
---
apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1
kind: Subscription
metadata:
  name: advanced-cluster-management
  namespace: open-cluster-management
spec:
  channel: release-2.9
  installPlanApproval: Automatic
  name: advanced-cluster-management
  source: redhat-operators
  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace
EOF
Subtask 1.3: Configure Local OpenShift Cluster
Since we're working with open-source tools, we'll use CodeReady Containers (CRC) to simulate an OpenShift environment.

Step 1: Install and configure CodeReady Containers

# Download CRC
cd $INSTALL_DIR
wget https://developers.redhat.com/content-gateway/rest/mirror/pub/openshift-v4/clients/crc/latest/crc-linux-amd64.tar.xz
tar -xf crc-linux-amd64.tar.xz
sudo mv crc-linux-*/crc /usr/local/bin/
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/crc

# Set up CRC
crc setup
crc config set memory 16384
crc config set cpus 6
Step 2: Start the local OpenShift cluster

# Start CRC (this may take 10-15 minutes)
crc start

# Get cluster credentials
crc console --credentials

# Login to the cluster
eval $(crc oc-env)
oc login -u kubeadmin -p $(crc console --credentials | grep kubeadmin | awk '{print $3}')
Subtask 1.4: Deploy Advanced Cluster Management Hub
Step 1: Install the ACM operator

# Apply the ACM installation configuration
oc apply -f $INSTALL_DIR/config/acm-install.yaml

# Wait for the operator to be ready
oc wait --for=condition=Ready pod -l name=advanced-cluster-management-operator -n open-cluster-management --timeout=300s
Step 2: Create MultiClusterHub instance

# Create MultiClusterHub configuration
cat > $INSTALL_DIR/config/multiclusterhub.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: operator.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: MultiClusterHub
metadata:
  name: multiclusterhub
  namespace: open-cluster-management
spec:
  availabilityConfig: Basic
  enableClusterBackup: false
  imagePullSecret: ""
  ingress:
    sslCiphers:
    - ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384
    - ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384
    - ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256
    - ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256
  separateCertificateManagement: false
EOF

# Apply the configuration
oc apply -f $INSTALL_DIR/config/multiclusterhub.yaml
Step 3: Verify the installation

# Check the status of MultiClusterHub
oc get multiclusterhub -n open-cluster-management

# Wait for all components to be ready
oc wait --for=condition=Complete multiclusterhub/multiclusterhub -n open-cluster-management --timeout=1200s

# Verify all pods are running
oc get pods -n open-cluster-management
Task 2: Connect Multiple Clusters to the Platform Plus Instance
Subtask 2.1: Prepare Managed Cluster Environments
For this lab, we'll simulate additional clusters using Kind (Kubernetes in Docker).

Step 1: Install Kind on the system

# Install Kind
curl -Lo ./kind https://kind.sigs.k8s.io/dl/v0.20.0/kind-linux-amd64
sudo mv ./kind /usr/local/bin/kind
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/kind
Step 2: Create simulated managed clusters

# Create first managed cluster configuration
cat > $INSTALL_DIR/config/cluster1-config.yaml << 'EOF'
kind: Cluster
apiVersion: kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4
name: managed-cluster-1
nodes:
- role: control-plane
  kubeadmConfigPatches:
  - |
    kind: InitConfiguration
    nodeRegistration:
      kubeletExtraArgs:
        node-labels: "cluster=managed-cluster-1"
EOF

# Create second managed cluster configuration
cat > $INSTALL_DIR/config/cluster2-config.yaml << 'EOF'
kind: Cluster
apiVersion: kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4
name: managed-cluster-2
nodes:
- role: control-plane
  kubeadmConfigPatches:
  - |
    kind: InitConfiguration
    nodeRegistration:
      kubeletExtraArgs:
        node-labels: "cluster=managed-cluster-2"
EOF

# Create the clusters
kind create cluster --config $INSTALL_DIR/config/cluster1-config.yaml
kind create cluster --config $INSTALL_DIR/config/cluster2-config.yaml
Subtask 2.2: Import Managed Clusters
Step 1: Create ManagedCluster resources

# Switch back to the hub cluster context
oc config use-context crc-admin

# Create ManagedCluster for cluster 1
cat > $INSTALL_DIR/config/managed-cluster-1.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: ManagedCluster
metadata:
  name: managed-cluster-1
  labels:
    cloud: auto-detect
    vendor: auto-detect
    environment: development
spec:
  hubAcceptsClient: true
EOF

# Create ManagedCluster for cluster 2
cat > $INSTALL_DIR/config/managed-cluster-2.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: ManagedCluster
metadata:
  name: managed-cluster-2
  labels:
    cloud: auto-detect
    vendor: auto-detect
    environment: production
spec:
  hubAcceptsClient: true
EOF

# Apply the configurations
oc apply -f $INSTALL_DIR/config/managed-cluster-1.yaml
oc apply -f $INSTALL_DIR/config/managed-cluster-2.yaml
Step 2: Generate and apply import manifests

# Create import secrets for managed clusters
oc get secret -n managed-cluster-1 managed-cluster-1-import -o yaml > $INSTALL_DIR/config/cluster1-import.yaml
oc get secret -n managed-cluster-2 managed-cluster-2-import -o yaml > $INSTALL_DIR/config/cluster2-import.yaml

# Apply import manifests to managed clusters
kubectl config use-context kind-managed-cluster-1
kubectl apply -f $INSTALL_DIR/config/cluster1-import.yaml

kubectl config use-context kind-managed-cluster-2
kubectl apply -f $INSTALL_DIR/config/cluster2-import.yaml
Subtask 2.3: Configure Cluster Sets and Bindings
Step 1: Create ManagedClusterSets

# Switch back to hub cluster
oc config use-context crc-admin

# Create development cluster set
cat > $INSTALL_DIR/config/dev-clusterset.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1beta2
kind: ManagedClusterSet
metadata:
  name: development
spec:
  clusterSelector:
    selectorType: ExclusiveClusterSetLabel
EOF

# Create production cluster set
cat > $INSTALL_DIR/config/prod-clusterset.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: cluster.open-cluster-management.io/v1beta2
kind: ManagedClusterSet
metadata:
  name: production
spec:
  clusterSelector:
    selectorType: ExclusiveClusterSetLabel
EOF

# Apply cluster sets
oc apply -f $INSTALL_DIR/config/dev-clusterset.yaml
oc apply -f $INSTALL_DIR/config/prod-clusterset.yaml
Step 2: Assign clusters to cluster sets

# Label clusters for cluster set assignment
oc label managedcluster managed-cluster-1 cluster.open-cluster-management.io/clusterset=development
oc label managedcluster managed-cluster-2 cluster.open-cluster-management.io/clusterset=production

# Verify cluster assignments
oc get managedcluster --show-labels
Task 3: Verify Installation Through the Web Interface
Subtask 3.1: Access the OpenShift Platform Plus Console
Step 1: Get console URL and credentials

# Get the console URL
echo "Console URL: $(oc get route multicloud-console -n open-cluster-management -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')"

# Get admin credentials
echo "Username: kubeadmin"
echo "Password: $(crc console --credentials | grep kubeadmin | awk '{print $3}')"
Step 2: Access the web interface

Open a web browser and navigate to the console URL
Accept any SSL certificate warnings (this is expected in a lab environment)
Log in using the kubeadmin credentials
You should see the OpenShift Platform Plus dashboard
Subtask 3.2: Navigate and Verify Cluster Management Features
Step 1: Explore the Clusters view

In the console, navigate to Infrastructure > Clusters
Verify that you can see:
The local hub cluster
managed-cluster-1 (should show as "Ready")
managed-cluster-2 (should show as "Ready")
Click on each cluster to view detailed information
Step 2: Verify Cluster Sets

Navigate to Infrastructure > Cluster sets
Confirm you can see:
development cluster set with managed-cluster-1
production cluster set with managed-cluster-2
Click on each cluster set to view member clusters
Subtask 3.3: Test Application Deployment Across Clusters
Step 1: Create a sample application

# Create application namespace
oc create namespace sample-app

# Create a simple deployment
cat > $INSTALL_DIR/config/sample-app.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: hello-openshift
  namespace: sample-app
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: hello-openshift
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: hello-openshift
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: hello-openshift
        image: openshift/hello-openshift:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: hello-openshift
  namespace: sample-app
spec:
  selector:
    app: hello-openshift
  ports:
  - port: 8080
    targetPort: 8080
  type: ClusterIP
EOF

# Apply the application
oc apply -f $INSTALL_DIR/config/sample-app.yaml
Step 2: Create Application Set for multi-cluster deployment

# Create ApplicationSet configuration
cat > $INSTALL_DIR/config/applicationset.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: ApplicationSet
metadata:
  name: hello-openshift-appset
  namespace: open-cluster-management
spec:
  generators:
  - clusters:
      selector:
        matchLabels:
          environment: development
  template:
    metadata:
      name: '{{name}}-hello-openshift'
    spec:
      project: default
      source:
        repoURL: https://github.com/openshift/hello-openshift
        targetRevision: HEAD
        path: .
      destination:
        server: '{{server}}'
        namespace: sample-app
      syncPolicy:
        automated:
          prune: true
          selfHeal: true
        syncOptions:
        - CreateNamespace=true
EOF

# Apply the ApplicationSet (if Argo CD is available)
oc apply -f $INSTALL_DIR/config/applicationset.yaml
